@node	0.8, Chapter 1, 0.7, Chapter 0
@section Reading this book

This section covers the interaction convention, how exercises map to
SICP's numbers, the signpost sentences, and the exercise map. It is the
last primer section; the convention it fixes applies from the first
listing of @ref{0.2} and never changes afterward.

@heading The interaction convention

Each interaction in the book is one @code{@@example typescript} block: the
call, rendered in TypeScript, followed by its result as a comment line
carrying a @code{=>} marker.

@example typescript
5 + 3 + 4
// => 12

const size = 2;

5 + 3 + size
// => 10
@end example

@noindent
The marker keeps every result a valid comment in the source and readable
in the unhighlighted PDF, and the same value is asserted in the listing's
file under @file{packages/ch0/src/}, so the transcript cannot drift from
the code. Lines the book's programs print rather than evaluate, a probe
or a table driven by a loop, appear as comment lines without the marker.
A definition carries no response at all: it binds a name rather than
answering with a value. Every expression does answer, and the answer
follows in the marker form whatever its type:

@example typescript
const a = 3;

const b = a + 1;

a === b
// => false
@end example

@noindent
From chapter 4 on, where the book's programs are values of the
evaluator's @code{Value} type, a transcript shows the same shape with the
evaluator's printed forms. Nothing in a transcript is hand-copied: every
result in every listing is asserted by the file the listing names.

@heading How exercises map to SICP's numbers

Exercise numbers are one to one with SICP. An exercise whose material
survives in TypeScript keeps its number and its intent; only its language
changes. When a statement exists to teach the original language itself,
the edition replaces it under the same number and says so. Each exercise row in the
edition's map carries one of three classes: @code{T} for a translation
where only the syntax changes, @code{A} for an adaptation where the idea
survives and the statement changes, and @code{R} for a replacement that
keeps the number but teaches the section's material on host terms. The
map is the file @file{docs/exercise-map.md} at the repository root, one
table per section.

Two fixed sentences appear in the text wherever the numbering needs
explaining, and they always read exactly as follows. A replacement says:

@quotation
@emph{This edition replaces SICP exercise @var{N.M} with a host-language
exercise that keeps its number and teaching objective.}
@end quotation

@noindent
and an addition, numbered as the exercise it extends plus a lowercase
letter, says:

@quotation
@emph{Exercise @var{N.Ma} is added by this edition and extends exercise
@var{N.M}; SICP numbers stop at @var{N.M}.}
@end quotation

@noindent
At most one such addition exists per section per edition. The primer's
own exercises, 0.1 through 0.5, are this edition's additions in the same
spirit: they use only what @ref{0.2} through @ref{0.7} have taught, and
each one lives in the section that teaches its material, which is why the
recursion probe of exercise 0.3 sits in @ref{0.3} rather than at the end
of the chapter.

@heading A worked example

The three sessions of exercise 0.1 become TypeScript transcripts of the
convention's shape. The arithmetic session, evaluated in order:

@example typescript
486
// => 486

100
// => 100

5 + 3 + 4
// => 12

10 - 9
// => 1

2 * 4 + (4 - 6)
// => 6
@end example

@noindent
and the @code{square} session of that exercise, which needs a definition
first:

@example typescript
const square = (x: number): number => x * x;

square(21)
// => 441

square(2 + 5)
// => 49

square(square(3))
// => 81
@end example

@noindent
The full transcript, computed and asserted, is
@file{packages/ch0/src/08-reading-this-book.ts}. With the convention, the
numbering, and the signposts in hand, chapter 1 begins: @ref{1.1} builds
abstractions with procedures, and from its first listing every construct
will be one this primer has already shown.

